        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm trying to trace the derivation through the dhātu \"dheṭ\" with the uṇādi pratyaya \"na,\" checking which specific uṇādi sūtra governs forming \"dhenā\" from this root.\n\nI'm trying to recall the Kāśikā gloss for \"धापॄवस्यज्यतिभ्यो नः,\" checking whether dhāna derives from dheṭ via the sandhi rule that turns dhe into dhā in upadeśa, alongside parṇa, vasna, and the other examples in that sūtra.\n\nContinuing through the sūtras, I'm now fairly confident I've located it: \"धेट इच्च\" (उ ३.११), giving धेना meaning river or speech, which fits the Vedic usage.\n\nChecking the commentary tradition, dhā with dheṭ seems less standard than I thought, but the \"धेट इच्च\" rule pointing to धेना via dheṭ with na feels right. I'll settle on धेना as the answer.\n\n",
